ry observations\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the circle \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\).\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(O,P,H\\) and\n\n\\[\nOP = PH . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PH\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – The centre of the circumcircle of \\(B\\,E\\,F\\) is \\(O\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) by construction.  \nBecause \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nME = MC = r ,\\qquad NF = ND = R . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(PA = PC = PD\\); hence the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=MA^{2}-r^{2}=PA^{2}-r^{2},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=NA^{2}-R^{2}=PA^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the power–product formula gives\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A),\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AP = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equalities in (4) and using (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP - AF\\cdot AP = (PA^{2}-r^{2})-(PA^{2}-R^{2}) = R^{2}-r^{2}=ME^{2}-NF^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(ME=MC\\) and \\(NF=ND\\), the right–hand side equals \\(MC^{2}-ND^{2}\\), which is precisely the power of the point \\(M\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) minus the power of the point \\(N\\) with respect to the same circle.  Hence\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP - AF\\cdot AP = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{P}}(M)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{P}}(N). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{P}}(M)=PM^{2}-PA^{2}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{P}}(N)=PN^{2}-PA^{2}\\); therefore (5) rewrites as\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP - AF\\cdot AP = PM^{2}-PN^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that the points \\(B,E,F\\) are the intersections of the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma,\\Gamma_{P}\\).  Consequently the radical centre of these three circles is the unique point that has equal power with respect to each of them; that point is precisely the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PH\\) (by (2) the powers of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal, and by (1) the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) is also the same).  Hence \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\) and also on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\); therefore \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 –  \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two triangles \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\).  \nBoth share the side \\(AP\\); moreover, by (1) we have\n\n\\[\nPE = PA = PF .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\) are isosceles with equal legs, and the angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEP = \\angle AFP . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow look at the triangles \\(\\triangle BPE\\) and \\(\\triangle BPF\\).  \nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the equalities (3) give\n\n\\[\n\\angle PBE = \\angle PBF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) and (8) we deduce that the pairs of triangles \\((\\triangle APE,\\triangle BPE)\\) and \\((\\triangle APF,\\triangle BPF)\\) are similar with the same orientation.  Consequently the ratios of the corresponding sides are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{BE}= \\frac{AF}{BF}= \\frac{PE}{PB}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nRelation (9) is precisely the defining property of an **external symmedian** of a triangle: a line through a vertex (here the vertex \\(B\\)) is an external symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Since the line \\(AP\\) passes through the vertex \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) and the centre of that circle is \\(O\\), the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the line \\(AP\\) meets the circle again at the point that is the external symmedian through \\(B\\)), we conclude that \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 3 –  Through the orthocentre, a line parallel to an external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcentre \\(O\\) and orthocentre \\(H\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be an external symmedian of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) issuing from the vertex \\(X\\).  \nDenote by \\(t\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(s\\).  \n\nBecause the isogonal conjugate of a symmedian is a median, the isogonal of \\(s\\) is the internal median from \\(X\\) to the side \\(YZ\\).  The isogonal of the line \\(t\\) (which passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(s\\)) is therefore the line through the circumcentre \\(O\\) parallel to the median from \\(X\\).  But the only line through \\(O\\) that is parallel to a median of the triangle is the **tangent** to the circumcircle at the point where the median meets the circle.  Hence the isogonal of \\(t\\) is a tangent; consequently \\(t\\) itself is a tangent (the isogonal map preserves tangency). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 1 the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) has centre \\(O\\), the midpoint of \\(PH\\).  \nLemma 2 tells us that the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nApplying Lemma 3 to the triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with orthocentre \\(H\\) we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the external symmedian \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. ∎\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
