allel to an external symmedian is tangent to the circum‑circle.  \n   The lemma is proved by an elementary isogonal‑conjugate argument: the isogonal of an external symmedian is a median; the isogonal of a line through the orthocentre parallel to the symmedian is a line through the circum‑centre parallel to the median, i.e. a tangent at the point where the median meets the circum‑circle. Since isogonal conjugation preserves tangency, the original line is also tangent.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives that \\(\\omega\\) is centred at \\(O\\); Lemma 2 tells us that \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); Lemma 3 applied to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (whose orthocentre is exactly the point \\(H\\) of the original configuration) yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\). ∎  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence the three points \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the circle \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(PH\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(P,O,H\\) and\n\n\\[\nOP=OH= \\tfrac12 PH . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(O\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); in particular\n\n\\[\nOM=ON=OP. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 –  The centre of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is \\(O\\)\n\nConsider the three circles  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\));  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\));  \n* \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) (centre \\(P\\), radius \\(PA\\)).\n\nThe points \\(B,E,F\\) are respectively the second intersections of the pairs\n\\((\\Omega,\\Gamma),(\\Omega,\\Gamma_{P}),(\\Gamma,\\Gamma_{P})\\); therefore the three\nradical axes of the circles are the lines \\(AB,AE,AF\\).  \nAll three radical axes meet at the common point \\(A\\); hence the **radical centre**\nof the three circles is the point \\(A\\).\n\nLet \\(X\\) be any point that has equal power with respect to the three circles.\nBecause the power of a point with respect to a circle is a quadratic function,\nthe set of points having equal power to two given circles is a line (the radical\naxis).  Consequently the radical centre is the unique point belonging to the\nthree radical axes, i.e. the point \\(A\\).\n\nNow we compute the power of the point \\(O\\) with respect to the three circles.\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Omega\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(O)=OM^{2}-r^{2}=OP^{2}-r^{2}. \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Gamma\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(O)=ON^{2}-R^{2}=OP^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{P}}(O)=OP^{2}-PA^{2}=0 \\qquad\\text{by (1)}. \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\nSubtracting (5) from (4) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(O)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(O)=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, using (1) we have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=MA^{2}-r^{2}=PA^{2}-r^{2},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=NA^{2}-R^{2}=PA^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nso that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=R^{2}-r^{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nComparing (7) and (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(O)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(O)\n=\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A).\n\\]\n\nSince the power difference of two circles is constant along any line parallel\nto the line joining their centres, the equality above implies that the\ndifferences of the powers of \\(O\\) and of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\) are the same.  Together with (6) this shows that \\(O\\) has the same\npower with respect to the three circles as the radical centre \\(A\\); consequently\n\\(O\\) also lies on the three radical axes, i.e. on the lines \\(AB,AE,AF\\).\n\nThus \\(O\\) is equidistant from the three points \\(B,E,F\\); therefore \\(O\\) is the\ncircum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 –  \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) and on the line\n\\(AP\\), the power–product formula applied at \\(A\\) gives\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP = MA^{2}-r^{2}, \\qquad\nAF\\cdot AP = NA^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we rewrite the right–hand sides as\n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=PA^{2}+r^{2},\\qquad\nNA^{2}=PA^{2}+R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence (9) becomes\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP = PA^{2},\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AP = PA^{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(AE=AF\\).  Moreover, from (1) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nPE = PA = PF . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the two triangles \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\).  \nBoth are isosceles with equal legs (11) and share the base \\(AP\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEP = \\angle AFP . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nNext look at the triangles \\(\\triangle BPE\\) and \\(\\triangle BPF\\).  \nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), we have\n\n\\[\nMB = r,\\qquad NB = R .\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of sines in \\(\\triangle MBP\\) and \\(\\triangle NBP\\) and the equalities\n\\(MP=NP\\) (both equal to \\(OP\\) by (3)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle PBE}{\\sin\\angle PBF}= \\frac{MB}{NB}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBut from (12) we also have \\(\\angle PBE = \\angle PBF\\); hence (13) forces\n\\(r=R\\).  The