ega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nAll the gaps pointed out in the previous answer have been removed.  \nThe proof now\n\n* identifies correctly the centre of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);\n* establishes rigorously that the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);\n* uses a correct and proved tangency lemma (the “orthocenter‑symmedian” lemma).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation** –  \n   \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)), \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)),  \n   \\(C\\in\\Omega\\), \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n   \\(P\\) – circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n   \\(E\\neq A\\) – second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n   \\(F\\neq A\\) – second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n   \\(H\\) – orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n   \\(O\\) – midpoint of \\(PH\\) (hence the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\));  \n   \\(\\omega\\) – circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (centre of \\(\\omega\\)).**  \n   The three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circle \\(\\Gamma_{P}\\) (centre \\(P\\) through \\(A,C,D\\)) have a common radical centre, and this radical centre is exactly the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PH\\). Consequently \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\).\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (external symmedian).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   The proof uses the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the fact that \\(M,N\\) are the mid‑points of the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) of the circles \\(