n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \nMoreover, \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) iff \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is **orthogonal** to the line \\(AP\\) (a basic property of inversion: a line not through the centre is mapped to a circle through the centre, and the angle between the line and the centre‑line equals the angle between the image circle and the centre‑line).  \n\nHence \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) that is **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\) at \\(A\\) and passes through the image of \\(H\\), i.e. through \\(H'\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell^{*}= \\text{the circle }(A H' )\\text{ having centre on the line }AP\\text{ and radius }AH'.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H'\\in AB\\) by (3), the circle \\((A H')\\) is the *Apollonius circle* of the segment \\(AB\\) corresponding to the ratio \\(AH':HB\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWe now prove a key fact that will replace the erroneous Lemma 1 of the previous attempt.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  In \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the line \\(AP\\) (through the vertex \\(B\\)) is the external symmedian.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply the intersecting‑chords theorem in the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AE = AM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nAB\\cdot AF = AN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=AM^{2}-r^{2}=AN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (6) into (5) yields\n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AE = AB\\cdot AF = PA^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AE=AF .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow apply the intersecting‑chords theorem to the line \\