 Lemma 5 (a Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle) yields the desired tangency: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R\\),  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r\\) (\\(R<r\\)).  \n\nThe circles intersect at two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a (non‑trivial) homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, the ratio of the radii is \\(k=\\dfrac{r}{R}>1\\); the image of any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is the point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) such that \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\).  \n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) we obtain a point \\(C'\\in\\Gamma\\) with  \n\n\\[\nAC'=k\\cdot AC= \\frac{r}{R}\\,AC .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), the ray \\(AC\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) of the ray \\(AM\\); consequently the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\).  \nHence \\(C'\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The point \\(P\\) and the angle \\(\\angle MPN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with bases \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APC = \\tfrac12\\angle CAD ,\\qquad\n\\angle DPA = \\angle APD = \\tfrac12\\angle CAD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(CMND\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CMD = 180^{\\circ} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle MPN = 180^{\\circ} - 2\\angle CAD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) and its reflection \\(H'\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nA classical property of the orthocentre states that the reflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides of a triangle lie on its circum‑circle.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN) .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause the reflection in \\(MN\\) sends a line parallel to \\(MN\\) onto itself and sends a line perpendicular to \\(MN\\) onto a line symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), the image of the line \\(AP\\) (which is not parallel to \\(MN\\) in general) is a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the Simson line of the point \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The Simson line of \\(H'\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nRecall the definition of the Simson line: for a point \\(X\\) on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the three sides are collinear; this line is called the Simson line of \\(X\\).\n\nApply this to the point \\(X=H'\\) on \\((PMN)\\).  \nLet \\(U,V,W\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(H'\\) to \\(PM,\\,MN,\\,NP\\) respectively.  \nBy the Simson property, \\(U,V,W\\) are collinear; we have already identified this line as the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nNow observe that the triangle formed by the three feet \\(U,V,W\\) is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nIndeed, the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and its centre lies on the line \\(AP\\).  \nMoreover, the right angles \\(\\angle BEA\\) and \\(\\angle BFA\\) (each subtended by the diameter \\(AB\\) of the common chord) imply that \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are respectively perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and to the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\).  \n\nConsequently the three lines \\(BE,\\,BF,\\) and the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(MN\\) are respectively parallel to the three sides \\(PM,\\,MN,\\,NP\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nTherefore the pedal triangle of \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with centre \\(B\\).  \n\nA well‑known theorem about Simson lines states that the Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle of that point.  \nApplying this theorem to the point \\(H'\\) and the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the Simson line of \\(H'\\)) is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle of \\(H'\\).  \nBecause this pedal triangle is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the two circum‑circles are also homothetic; the homothety sends the point of tangency to a point of tangency.  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(H'\\) (the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\)) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* The Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle of that point.  \n* The pedal triangle of \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently their circum‑circles are homothetic, and the tangency is preserved under the homothety.\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was required.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
