the orthocentre states that the reflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides of a triangle lie on its circum‑circle.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN),\\qquad\\text{the circum‑circle of }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe reflection in the line \\(MN\\) sends a line making an angle \\(\\theta\\) with \\(MN\\) to a line making the same angle \\(\\theta\\) on the opposite side; in particular it sends the line \\(AP\\) to a line through \\(H\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\).  \nThus the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\n\\]\n\nis the image of \\(AP\\) under the reflection in \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(\\ell_H\\) is the Simson line of \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor a point \\(X\\) on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the three sides are collinear; this line is called the **Simson line** of \\(X\\).\n\nTake \\(X=H'\\) (which belongs to \\((PMN)\\) by (3)).  \nLet \\(U,V,W\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(H'\\) to the sides \\(PM,\\,MN,\\,NP\\) respectively.  \nBy the Simson property, \\(U,V,W\\) are collinear; the line \\(UVW\\) is the Simson line of \\(H'\\).\n\nBecause the reflection in \\(MN\\) maps the line \\(AP\\) onto \\(\\ell_H\\) and maps each side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) onto a line parallel to itself, the three feet \\(U,V,W\\) lie on \\(\\ell_H\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H\\;=\\; \\text{Simson line of }H'\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Relation with the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at a point \\(F\\).  \nThus the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the two given circles; consequently the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic.  \nMoreover the centre of this circle lies on the line \\(AP\\) (the line joining the centres of the two intersecting circles passes through the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\), which is precisely the point where the line \\(AP\\) meets the radical axis of the two circles).\n\nObserve the following parallelisms:\n\n* \\(BE\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) because \\(BE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) whose endpoints are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\) (the line of centres).  \n  Hence \\(BE\\parallel PM\\) (the side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) opposite \\(N\\)).\n\n* Similarly \\(BF\\parallel PN\\).\n\n* The line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(MN\\) is obviously parallel to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nTherefore the triangle formed by the three lines \\(BE,\\;BF,\\) and the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(MN\\) is **homothetic** to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with centre \\(B\\).  \nConsequently the pedal triangle of \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (whose vertices are the feet \\(U,V,W\\)) is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\); the homothety sends the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle onto the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Simson‑line tangency theorem\n\n**Theorem (Simson‑line tangency).**  \nLet \\(\\Delta\\) be a triangle and let \\(X\\) be a point on its circum‑circle.  \nDenote by \\(\\Pi_X\\) the pedal triangle of \\(X\\) (the triangle formed by the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the sides of \\(\\Delta\\)).  \nThen the Simson line of \\(X\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\Pi_X\\).\n\nApply this theorem to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(X=H'\\).  \nBy (4) the Simson line of \\(H'\\) is precisely \\(\\ell_H\\); by the theorem \\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle \\(UVW\\).  \n\nBecause the pedal triangle \\(UVW\\) is homothetic to \\(\\triangle BEF\\