\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a (non‑trivial) homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_A:\\Omega\\longrightarrow\\Gamma ,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_A(X)=A+\\frac{r}{R}\\,\\overrightarrow{AX},\n\\]\n\nwith centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k=r/R>1\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) the image \\(X'=\\mathcal H_A(X)\\) lies on the ray \\(AX\\) and satisfies \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\).\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) to the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) we obtain a point  \n\n\\[\nC'=\\mathcal H_A(C)\\in\\Gamma ,\\qquad AC'=k\\cdot AC .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), the ray \\(AC\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) of the ray \\(AM\\); consequently the image of the whole line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(A\\) **parallel** to \\(MN\\).  \nThus \\(C'\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Position of \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore it is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nIn particular the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\).\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\in MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is contained in the line \\(MN\\); its perpendicular bisector is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nP\\in \\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\perp MN .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(P\\notin MN\\); the triangle \\(PMN\\) is non‑degenerate and possesses a well‑defined orthocentre \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its reflection \\(H'\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n\nA standard property of the orthocentre states that the reflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides of a triangle lie on its circum‑circle.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN),\\qquad\\text{the circum‑circle of }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe reflection in the line \\(MN\\) sends a line making an angle \\(\\theta\\) with \\(MN\\) to a line making the same angle \\(\\theta\\) on the opposite side; in particular it sends the line \\(AP\\) to a line through \\(H\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\).  \nThus the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\n\\]\n\nis the image of \\(AP\\) under the reflection in \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(\\ell_H\\) is the Simson line of \\(H'\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor a point \\(X\\) on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the three sides are collinear; this line is called the **Simson line** of \\(X\\).\n\nTake \\(X=H'\\) (which belongs to \\((PMN)\\) by (3)).  \nLet \\(U,V,W\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(H'\\) to the sides \\(PM,\\,MN,\\,NP\\) respectively.  \nBy the Simson property, \\(U,V,W\\) are collinear; the line \\(UVW\\) is the Simson line of \\(H'\\).\n\nBecause the reflection in \\(MN\\) maps the line \\(AP\\) onto \\(\\ell_H\\) and maps each side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) onto a line parallel to itself, the three feet \\(U,V,W\\) lie on \\(\\ell_H\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H\\;=\\; \\text{Simson line of }H'\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Relation with the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at a point \\(F\\).  \nThus the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the two given circles; consequently the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is c