}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  A right‑angle relation that **does not** use a false cyclic‑diameter claim  \n\nWe now prove the identity  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ABF+\\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (by (4)).  Hence the line \\(AP\\) is the **internal angle bisector** of \\(\\angle CAD\\).\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\angle CAD\\) to \\(\\angle FAN\\); because \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(A\\) to itself, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = \\angle FAN .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle FAN = \\angle BFA\\) (the points \\(A,F,N\\) are collinear with \\(B\\) lying on the same circle \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSimilarly, \\(\\angle DAC = \\angle ABF\\).  Since \\(\\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle CAD + \\angle DAC = \\angle 