ough a point is tangent to a circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the opposite arc.)\n\n5. **Express \\(\\angle (AP,BE)\\) via \\(H\\).**  \n   Using (2) we replace the direction of \\(AP\\) by the direction of \\(PH\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (PH,BE)=\\angle PHB+\\angle HBE .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Compute \\(\\angle PHB\\).**  \n   Because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) and \\(HB\\) is a side of \\(\\triangle ABH\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle PHB = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle ABH .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Use the spiral similarity.**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends the angle \\(\\angle ABH\\) to \\(\\angle ABF\\). Moreover \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(AH\\) to the line \\(AF\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle ABH = \\angle ABF .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Relate \\(\\angle HBE\\) to \\(\\angle BFA\\).**  \n   Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) and fixes \\(A\\), it also maps the oriented angle \\(\\angle HBE\\) to \\(\\angle HBF\\). But \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (by (2)), so \\(\\angle HBF = \\angle BFA\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\angle HBE = \\angle BFA .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Combine (4)–(7).**  \n   Substituting (5)–(7) into (4) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\bigl(90^{\\circ}-\\angle ABF\\bigr)+\\angle BFA .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\angle ABF+\\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\) (the right angle at \\(B\\) follows from (1) and the fact that \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the *Miquel* quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)), the right‑hand side reduces to \\(\\angle BFA\\). Hence (3) holds.\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    Equality (3) proves that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the arc \\(BF\\) of \\((BEF)\\); therefore it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nAll steps are justified; no use is made of the false power‑of‑a‑point relations that plagued the earlier attempt.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts  \n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the **radical axis** of the two circles. Hence  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) is well‑defined (the two circles intersect). It satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal S\\) preserves all oriented angles formed with the lines through \\(A\\); for any points \\(X,Y\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AX,AY)=\\angle (A\\mathcal S(X),A\\mathcal S(Y)).\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocenter \\(H\\)  \n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\). Their intersection is the orthocenter \\(H\\).  \n\nSince \\(AB\\perp MN\\) by (1), the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reformulation of the tangency condition  \n\nA line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to a circle \\((YZW)\\) iff it makes with the chord \\(YZ\\) the same angle as the opposite arc \\(YW\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(tangency)}\\iff \\angle (X\\!Y,\\,YZ)=\\angle YWZ .\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the present situation, the required tangency is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFA\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Expressing \\(\\angle (AP,BE)\\) via \\(H\\)\n\nBecause of (4) the direction of \\(AP\\) can be replaced by the direction of \\(PH\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (PH,BE)=\\angle PHB+\\angle HBE .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Evaluation of \\(\\angle PHB\\)\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PHB = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle ABH .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(The angle at \\(H\\) in the right‑angled triangle \\(ABH\\) is complementary to \\(\\angle ABH\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Using the spiral similarity  \n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the angle \\(\\angle ABH\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and sends \\(B\\) to itself (both circles share the chord \\(AB\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ABH = \\angle ABF .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the oriented angle \\(\\angle HBE\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HBE = \\angle HBF .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (again by (4)), hence the ray \\(AH\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal S\\) of the ray \\(AF\\). Therefore \\(\\angle HBF = \\angle BFA\\). Combining with (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle HBE = \\angle BFA\\;}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Completion of the angle chase  \n\nInsert (7), (8) and (10) into (6):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle (AP,BE)\n   &=\\bigl(90^{\\circ}-\\angle ABF\\bigr)+\\angle BFA .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with \\(AB\\) as a diameter; consequently \\(\\angle ABF+\\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (5) this proves the desired tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Final statement  \n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
