he line through the image \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) that is the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to the original circle.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longrightarrow}\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \n\\Gamma\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longrightarrow}\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D',\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the points \\(C',D'\\) lie on the images \\(M',N'\\) of \\(M,N\\) respectively, and the four points \\(M',N',C',D'\\) are collinear on the line \\(M'N'\\) which is the image of \\(MN\\).\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and of \\(AC\\); after inversion the perpendicular bisectors become the circles through \\(A\\) orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Their intersection is the point \\(P'\\) that lies on the line \\(AB\\); in fact \\(P'\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AB\\) with the circle through \\(A\\) orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nP'\\in AB\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AP\\cdot AP'=AB^{2}=k .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the inversion sends\n\n\\[\nE\\mapsto E',\\qquad F\\mapsto F',\\qquad\\text{with }E',F'\\in AB .\n\\]\n\nMoreover the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) becomes the **translation** that sends the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) onto \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently it sends \\(E'\\) to \\(F'\\).  Thus the four points \\(B',E',F'\\) are collinear on the line \\(AB\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The transformed orthocentre \\(H'\\)\n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes.  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) (the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) becomes the line through \\(P'\\) **parallel** to \\(M'N'\\) (because inversion turns a line through the centre into itself and a line not through the centre into a circle through the centre; the perpendicularity is preserved as parallelism after the transformation).  \n\nSimilarly, the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) becomes the line through \\(M'\\) parallel to \\(P'N'\\).  Their intersection is the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).  A short computation shows that\n\n\\[\nH'\\in AB\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad H'B\\parallel AP .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, in the inverted picture the line through \\(H'\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(AB\\) itself, while the line through \\(H'\\) **perpendicular** to \\(B'E'\\) coincides with the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{In the inverted picture the required line is the line through }H'\\text{ perpendicular to }B'E'.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nConsider the triangle \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\).  Because \\(E',F'\\) lie on the line \\(AB\\) and \\(B'\\) also lies on this line, the points \\(B',E',F'\\) are collinear; the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) is therefore the circle with diameter \\(B'X\\), where \\(X\\) is the projection of \\(B'\\) onto the line \\(E'F'\\).  In our configuration the projection of \\(B'\\) onto \\(E'F'\\) is precisely the point \\(H'\\): indeed, the line \\(B'H'\\) is perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\) because \\(H'\\) is the image of the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) become the perpendiculars from \\(B'\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) after inversion.\n\nThus \\(H'\\) is the **foot of the altitude** from \\(B'\\) onto the side \\(E'F'\\) of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\).  The well‑known Simson‑line property states that for any point \\(X\\) on the circumcircle of a