le PMN\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\sigma(H_{1})=H\\).\n\n3. **The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is the common internal bisector of the angles formed by the two circles at \\(A\\); therefore the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) is the reflection of the first intersection \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) in the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\sigma(E)=F,\\qquad \\sigma(F)=E .\n   \\]\n\n4. **The circle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(P\\) and \\(H\\).**  \n   The quadrilateral \\(BCDE\\) is cyclic because \\(\\angle BCD=\\angle BAD\\) (both subtend the same arc of the two given circles).  \n   Its circumcenter is \\(Q\\) (the centre of \\((ABD)\\)), and the nine‑point circle of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) coincides with \\((BCD)\\).  \n   Since \\(Q\\) is the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\sigma\\), the image of \\((BCD)\\) under \\(\\sigma\\) is the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\); consequently \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(P\\).  \n   Moreover, the nine‑point circle of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) contains the orthocenter \\(H_{1}\\); applying \\(\\sigma\\) we obtain that \\((BEF)\\) also contains \\(\\sigma(H_{1})=H\\).  \n   Hence \\(P\\) and \\(H\\) lie on \\((BEF)\\).\n\n5. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   For any point \\(X\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle, the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the three sides are collinear; this line is the Simson line of \\(X\\).  \n   Because \\(H\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\), its Simson line with respect to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is well defined.  \n   The two feet on the sides \\(BE\\) and \\(EF\\) are symmetric with respect to the axis \\(AP\\) (the image under \\(\\sigma\\) of the symmetric feet on \\(BC\\) and \\(CD\\)).  \n   Therefore the Simson line of \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   A basic proper