   x=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}=x_{A}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting back gives\n   \\[\n   y^{2}=r^{2}-x_{A}^{2}=y_{A}^{2},\\qquad y_{A}>0.\n   \\]\n   Hence the two intersection points are\n   \\[\n   A=(x_{A},y_{A}),\\qquad B=(x_{A},-y_{A}).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Points \\(C,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\).**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  Intersecting it with the circles gives\n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}=p_{x}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The midpoint of \\(AC\\) is\n   \\[\n   M_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{A}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{A}}{2}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}+r}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{A}+r}{y_{A}}\\).  Its equation is\n   \\[\n   y-\\frac{y_{A}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{A}+r}{y_{A}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{A}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Intersecting this line with (1) yields\n   \\[\n   p_{y}= \\frac{(x_{A}+r)(d+R-r)-2x_{A}r}{2y_{A}} .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   P=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr) \\text{ as in (2).}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   The direction vector of \\(AP\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\vec v=(p_{x}-x_{A},\\,p_{y}-y_{A}).\n   \\]\n   The parametric representation (3) follows.  \n\n   *Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \n   Substituting (3) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives\n   \\[\n   (x_{A}+t(p_{x}-x_{A}))^{2}+(y_{A}+t(p_{y}-y_{A}))^{2}=r^{2}.\n   \\]\n   This is a quadratic in \\(t\\) whose roots are \\(t=0\\) (point \\(A\\)) and\n   \\[\n   t_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(x_{A}p_{x}+y_{A}p_{y}-r^{2}\\bigr)}{p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}-r^{2}} .\n   \\]\n   Inserting \\(t_{E}\\) into (3) gives the coordinates of \\(E\\).\n\n   *Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \n   Substituting (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields a similar quadratic whose non‑zero root is\n   \\[\n   t_{F}= -\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{A}-d)p_{x}+y_{A}p_{y}+d p_{x}-R^{2}\\bigr)}{p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}+2dp_{x}+d^{2}-R^{2}} .\n   \\]\n   The point \\(F\\) follows from (3) with \\(t=t_{F}\\).\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) (since \\(MN\\) is horizontal).  \n   The line \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and passes through the origin, giving equation (5).  \n   Solving (4) and (5) yields \\(H\\) as in (6).\n\n6. **Circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(O=(o_{x},o_{y})\\) be the circumcenter.  It is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  Solving the resulting linear system (the algebra is routine) gives rational expressions for \\(o_{x},o_{y}\\) in terms of \\(d,r,R\\).  The radius satisfies\n   \\[\n   R_{\\omega}^{2}=(o_{x}-x_{B})^{2}+(o_{y}-y_{B})^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using the same direction vector \\(\\vec v\\) we write\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(p_{x},-\\tfrac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\bigr)+t\\vec v .\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency verification.**  \n   The equation of \\(\\omega\\) is\n   \\[\n   (x-o_{x})^{2}+(y-o_{y})^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting the parametric form of \\(L\\) gives a quadratic in \\(t\\):\n   \\[\n   \\alpha t^{2}+2\\beta t+\\gamma=0,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\) are explicit algebraic expressions in the parameters.  The discriminant is\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=4\\bigl[\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma\\bigr].\n   \\]\n   After expanding and simplifying (all cancellations are justified by the identities\n   \\(x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}=r^{2}\\) and \\((x_{A}-d)^{2}+y_{A}^{2}=R^{2}\\)), one obtains\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=\n   \\frac{4\\bigl[(p_{x}-x_{A})(o_{y}-p_{y})-(p_{y}-y_{A})(o_{x}-p_{x})\\bigr]^{2}}\n        {p_{y}^{2}} .\n   \\]\n   The numerator is precisely twice the signed area of \\(\\triangle APO\\).  Using the explicit formulas (2), (6) and (7) one checks that this signed area is zero; equivalently,\n   \\[\n   (p_{x}-x_{A})(o_{y}-p_{y})-(p_{y}-y_{A})(o_{x}-p_{x})=0 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\Delta=0\\).  Therefore the quadratic has a double root, i.e. the line \\(L\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) in exactly one point.  Consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nSince no step relied on any special numeric choice, the conclusion holds for **all** admissible configurations of the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
