d line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The configuration and an inversion\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (\\(A\\neq B\\));\n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the nearer point to \\(M\\));\n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct because the circles intersect in two points and \\(r<R\\).\n\nPerform an **inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power** (any positive number will do).  \nDenote the images of the objects by a prime, e.g. \\(\\Omega'=\\mathcal I(\\Omega)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  What the inversion does\n\n*Because the centre of inversion lies on both circles, each circle is sent to a line.*\n\nIndeed, a circle passing through the centre of inversion is mapped to a line not passing through the centre, and conversely. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega' = \\ell_{1},\\qquad \\Gamma' = \\ell_{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) are two distinct lines intersecting at the image of the second common point \\(B\\); denote this intersection by \\(B'\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) lies on both circles, the image of \\(A\\) is the point at infinity in the direction orthogonal to both \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and