s parallel to both). Consequently **\\(H'\\) is the intersection of the altitudes from \\(M'\\) and \\(N'\\), i.e. the foot of the perpendicular from the point at infinity on \\(\\ell\\) to the line \\(M'N'\\)**; in other words, \\(H'\\) is the projection of any point of \\(\\ell\\) onto \\(M'N'\\).\n\nSummarising the transformed picture:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) intersect at \\(B'\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) is a line parallel to both \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\);\n* \\(C',D'\\) are the feet from the point at infinity on \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\);\n* \\(E',F'\\) are the intersections of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\);\n* \\(H'\\) is the foot from the point at infinity on \\(\\ell\\) to the line \\(M'N'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A convenient homothety\n\nAll the objects now lie in a configuration that is invariant under a homothety with centre \\(B'\\) sending the line \\(\\ell\\) to the line \\(M'N'\\). Indeed, because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\), the triangles formed by the three lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell\\) and by \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},M'N'\\) are homothetic with centre \\(B'\\). Under this homothety\n\n\\[\nE'\\mapsto E'',\\qquad F'\\mapsto F'',\\qquad H'\\mapsto H'',\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(E'',F''\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(B'\\) onto \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) (i.e. the same points \\(E',F'\\)), and \\(H''\\) is the foot from \\(B'\\) onto \\(M'N'\\). Hence **\\(H''\\) coincides with the orthogonal projection of \\(B'\\) onto the side \\(M'N'\\) of the triangle \\(\\triangle B'\\ell_{1}\\ell_{2}\\).**\n\nConsequently the line \\(H''B'\\) is the *Simson line* of the point \\(B'\\) with respect to the triangle formed by the three lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},M'N'\\). By the classical Simson theorem, this line passes through the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(B'\\) to the three sides, i.e. through \\(E',F'\\) and the f