he radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \n   Because the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends them to the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=AE\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=AF\\).  \n   Consequently the points \\(E,F\\) are fixed, while the other points of the configuration are transformed as follows:  \n\n   \\[\n   C\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;C^{*}\\in \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad   \n   D\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;D^{*}\\in \\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad   \n   P\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;P^{*},\\qquad   \n   M\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;M^{*},\\qquad   \n   N\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;N^{*},\\qquad   \n   H\\;\\xrightarrow{\\mathcal I}\\;H^{*}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **The image of the circumcenter \\(P\\).**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((ACD)\\) is centred at \\(P\\) and passes through \\(A\\).  \n   Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) a circle through the