P\\) coincides with \\(EF\\).\n\n6. **Parallelism of \\(EF\\) and \\(AP\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal.  \n   Hence \\(\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA\\).  \n   But \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\); the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are respectively perpendicular to the radii \\(ME\\) and \\(NF\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,AE)=\\angle (AP,AF)=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\angle CPA+\\angle DPA\\bigr)=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\angle CPA+\\angle CPA\\bigr)=\\angle CPA .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the ray \\(AP\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\); this is possible only when the two chords are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence the line \\(EF\\) (which joins the second intersection points of \\(AP\\) with the two circles) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   From step 5 we need the line through \\(A^{\\prime}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to be \\(EF\\).  \n   Step 6 shows that \\(EF\\parallel AP\\); consequently the unique line through \\(A^{\\prime}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly \\(EF\\).  \n   Undoing the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) we obtain that the line through the original orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required tangency holds for every admissible configuration. ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P