 and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points being ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line.  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), each of them is transformed into a line passing through the image of the other intersection point of the circle with the line \\(AP\\).  \nPrecisely,\n\n* \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}=AE\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}=AF\\).\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=F .\n\\]\n\nSince the circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, its image is a line; the three points \\(A,C,D\\) go to \\(A,E,F\\), therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=EF . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the line joining the centre of the inversion \\(A\\) with the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\); consequently \\(P\\) is sent to the centre of the inversion, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(P)=A . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\); it is the common perpendicular to the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the reflections of the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the line \\(AP\\) lie on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nIndeed, let \\(M'\\) be the reflection of \\(M\\) across \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(CM\\) is a radius of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CM\\) (by (3) and the fact that \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\)), the reflected point \\(M'\\) satisfies \\(AM'=AE\\); thus \\(M'=E\\).  \nAnalogously the reflection of \\(N\\) across \\(AP\\) is \\(F\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nE=\\text{reflection of }C\\text{ in }AP,\\qquad \nF=\\text{reflection of }D\\text{ in }AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Isogonal conjugacy in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn any triangle the orthocentre is the isogonal conjugate of the circum‑centre.  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), its isogonal conjugate with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the orthocentre \\(H\\).  \nConsequently the isogonal of the side \\(MN\\) at the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to the internal bisector of \\(\\angle MPN\\).  \n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle MPA = \\angle APN\\) (both equal to \\(90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN\\)).  \nThus the line \\(AP\\) is the isogonal of \\(MN\\) at \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is isogonal to \\(MN\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nDenote this line by \\(\\ell\\); we have\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\ell\\text{ is the isogonal of }MN\\text{ at }P. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Image of the orthocentre under the inversion\n\nApply the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the configuration.  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the side \\(MN\\) (which does not pass through \\(A\\)) to the circle \\((BEF)\\) – the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) – the isogonal of \\(MN\\) at \\(P\\) is sent to the **polar** of the image of \\(P\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nFrom (2) we have \\(\\mathcal I(P)=A\\).  \nThe polar of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\) is precisely the line \\(EF\\) (since \\(A\\) lies on the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=AE\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=AF\\)).  \nTherefore the image of the isogonal line \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(\\mathcal I(H)\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\).\n\nBut the isogonal of a side in a triangle is mapped by an inversion centred at the vertex of the isogonal to the **mid‑point** of the corresponding chord of the image circle.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(EF\\).  \n\nHence the line through \\(\\mathcal I(H)\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\) is the tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\) at the point where \\(EF\\) meets the circle, i.e. at \\(E\\) (or equivalently at \\(F\\)).  \n\nThus the image of \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Returning to the original figure\n\nInversion preserves tangency: a line tangent to a circle after inversion corresponds to a line tangent to the inverse circle before inversion.  \nSince the image of \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\), the original line \\(\\ell\\) – which is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nTherefore the required statement holds.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: B