us of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe proof shows that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(HP\\); then, using the spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends the segment \\(MN\\) onto the segment \\(EF\\), we prove that \\(HP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Pre‑liminaries** – From the definition of \\(P\\) we get \\(PA\\perp MN\\); because the radical axis of the two given circles is the line \\(AB\\) and is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we obtain the collinearity \\(A,B,P\\). Consequently the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\) is the line \\(PA\\); hence the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(PA\\) and the required line is exactly \\(HP\\).\n\n2. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)** – The equalities  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AM}=\\frac{AF}{AN},\\qquad \n   \\angle EAF=\\angle MAN\n   \\]\n\n   (which follow from the power‑of‑a‑point relations for the two original circles) guarantee the existence of a (directed) spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(M\\mapsto E\\) and \\(N\\mapsto F\\). Hence \\(\\sigma(MN)=EF\\) and \\(\\sigma(P)=P\\) because \\(P\\) lies on the common chord