e the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(HP\\).  \n\n---\n\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two original circles:\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AM = AF\\cdot AN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), so \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are the external segments of the secants \\(AP\\) through the circles, while \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are the radii to the points of tangency of the same secants.\n\nMoreover, the angles subtended by the arcs \\(MN\\) and \\(EF\\) at \\(A\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle EAF = \\angle MAN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(\\angle EAF\\) is the angle between the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) of the two circles, which equals the angle between the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) because each chord is symmetric with respect to the radius through its endpoint.\n\nEquations (2) and (3) are precisely the conditions for the existence of a (directed) spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(N\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(M)=E,\\qquad \\sigma(N)=F,\\qquad \\sigma(MN)=EF . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) lies on the common chord \\(AB\\), which is invariant under any similarity centred at \\(A\\) that maps the two circles onto each other, we have\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(P)=P . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma: image of an orthocenter under a spiral similarity\n\n*Lemma.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(\\tau\\) be a spiral similarity with centre \\(O\\) that sends side \\(XY\\) onto a segment \\(X'Y'\\). Then \\(\\tau\\) sends the orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) to the midpoint of \\(X'Y'\\).\n\n*Proof.* Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). By definition, the altitude from \\(X\\) is the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(YZ\\); after applying \\(\\tau\\) this 