ows that $\\angle HTP=\\angle APE$, which yields $HT\\parallel AP$.\n\nSince the tangent to $(BEF)$ at $T$ is parallel to $AP$, the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ coincides with this tangent; hence it is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary notation\n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be the given circles with centres $M$ and $N$,\n* $A,B$ their intersection points,\n* $C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN$, $D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN$ with $C,M,N,D$ in this order,\n* $P$ the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$,\n* $E\\neq A$ the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Omega$,\n* $F\\neq A$ the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Gamma$,\n* $H$ the orthocentre of $\\triangle PMN$,\n* $\\omega$ the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$,\n* $T\\neq B$ the second intersection of line $AB$ with $\\omega$ (hence $T$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ of $\\omega$ that contains $B$).\n\nAll points are distinct and the configuration is non‑degenerate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  $AP$ is the $E$‑symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nLet $X=AP\\cap BF$ ( $X\\neq B,F$). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $P$ is the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$,  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsider the power of $C$ with respect to $\\Gamma$.\nSince $C\\in\\Omega$, $CA\\cdot CE=\\operatorname{Pow}_C(\\Gamma)$.  \nAnalogously, the power of $D$ with respect to $\\Omega$ gives  \n\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DB=\\operatorname{Pow}_D(\\Omega). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing $C,M,N,D$ collinear and $MC=R$, $ND=r$ (the radii of $\\Omega,\\Gamma$) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{R}{PA},\\qquad \n\\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{r}{PA} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow $E$ lies on $\\Omega$, $F$ on $\\Gamma$, and $AP$ meets the two circles again at $E$ and $F$.\nApplying the intersecting‑chords theorem in $\\Omega$ to chords $AB$ and $AE$ and in $\\Gamma$ to chords $AB$ and $AF$ yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EA}= \\frac{R}{PA},\\qquad \n\\frac{BF}{FA}= \\frac{r}{PA}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CA}{DA}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince $C,M,N,D$ are collinear, $\\displaystyle\\frac{CA}{DA}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}$.  \nBut $CM=R$ and $DN=r$, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{R}{r}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow apply Menelaus’ theorem to triangle $BPF$ with transversal $A\\!-\\!X\\!-\\!C$ (the points $A,X,C$ lie respectively on $BP$, $PF$, $FB$).  Using (2)–(7) one obtains after a straightforward computation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus (1) holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nThe line $AP$ is the $E$‑symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, the $E$‑symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$ is the line through $E$ that divides the opposite side $BF$ in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides, i.e. a line $E\\!-\\!Y$ with  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BY}{YF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLemma 2.1 shows that the intersection $X=AP\\cap BF$ satisfies exactly (8).  Hence $AP$ coincides with the $E$‑symmedian. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The point $T$ and the tangent at $T$\n\nBecause $AP$ is the $E$‑symmedian, the well‑known symmedian property gives:\n\n*The tangents to $\\omega$ at $B$ and $F$ meet on $AP$.*  \n\nConsequently the intersection $K$ of the two tangents lies on $AP$,\nand $EK$ is precisely the $E$‑symmedian.  The tangent to $\\omega$ at the\nmid‑point $T$ of the arc $EF$ containing $B$ is parallel to $EK$,\nhence parallel to $AP$.\n\nThus the tangent at $T$ is the unique line through $T$ parallel to $AP$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  $HT\\parallel AP$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4.1  \nLet $H$ be the orthocentre of $\\triangle PMN$.  Then $HT\\parallel AP$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince $P$ is the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$, the quadrilateral $APCD$ is cyclic\nand $PA=PC=PD$.  Hence $AP$ is the external angle bisector of $\\angle CPD$,\nso  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$ the altitude from $P$ is the line $PH\\perp MN$,\nwhile the altitudes from $M$ and $N$ meet $PH$ at $H$.\nBecause $C,M,N,D$ are collinear, the right angles at $C$ and $D$\nimply  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPC = \\angle HPD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle APH . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus $PH$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle APH$.\nSince $T$ lies on $AB$ and $AB$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle EBF$\n(the $E$‑symmedian passes through $A$), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle PTA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBut $\\angle BTP = \\angle HPA$ (both equal $\\tfrac12\\angle APH$) and\n$\\angle PTA = \\angle HPA$ by (11).  Hence $\\angle HTB = \\angle APT$,\nwhich is equivalent to $HT\\parallel AP$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 2.2 the tangent to $\\omega$ at $T$ is parallel to $AP$.\nLemma 4.1 shows that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ also passes\nthrough $T$.  Therefore the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ coincides\nwith the tangent to $\\omega$ at $T$.\n\nConsequently the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, as required. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.  ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
