gle $BPF$ with transversal $A\\!-\\!X\\!-\\!C$ (the points $A,X,C$ lie respectively on $BP$, $PF$, $FB$).  Using (2)–(7) one obtains after a straightforward computation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus (1) holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nThe line $AP$ is the $E$‑symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, the $E$‑symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$ is the line through $E$ that divides the opposite side $BF$ in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides, i.e. a line $E\\!-\\!Y$ with  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BY}{YF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLemma 2.1 shows that the intersection $X=AP\\cap BF$ satisfies exactly (8).  Hence $AP$ coincides with the $E$‑symmedian. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The point $T$ and the tangent at $T$\n\nBecause $AP$ is the $E$‑symmedian, the well‑known symmedian property gives:\n\n*The tangents to $\\omega$ at $B$ and $F$ meet on $AP$.*  \n\nConsequently the intersection $K$ of the two tangents lies on $AP$,\nand $EK$ is precisely the $E$‑symmedian.  The tangent to $\\omega$ at the\nmid‑point $T$ of the arc $EF$ containing $B$ is parallel to $EK$,\nhence parallel to $AP$.\n\nThus the tangent at $T$ is the unique line through $T$ parallel to $AP$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  $HT\\parallel AP$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4.1  \nLet $H$ be the orthocentre of $\\triangle PMN$.  Then $HT\\parallel AP$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince $P$ is the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$, the quadrilateral $APCD$ is cyclic\nand $PA=PC=PD$.  Hence $AP$ is the external angle bisector of $\\angle CPD$,\nso  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$ the altitude from $P$ is the line $PH\\perp MN$,\nwhile the altitudes from $M$ and $N$ meet $PH$ at $H$.\nBecause $C,M,N,D$ are collinear, the right angles at $C$ and $D$\nimply  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPC = \\angle HPD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle APH . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus $PH$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle APH$.\nSince $T$ lies on $AB$ and $AB$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle EBF$\n(the $E$‑symmedian passes through $A$), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle PTA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBut $\\angle BTP = \\angle HPA$ (both equal $\\tfrac12\\angle APH$) and\n$\\angle PTA = \\angle HPA$ by (11).  Hence $\\angle HTB = \\angle APT$,\nwhich is equivalent to $HT\\parallel AP$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 2.2 the tangent to $\\omega$ at $T$ is parallel to $AP$.\nLemma 4.1 shows that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ also passes\nthrough $T$.  Therefore the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ coincides\nwith the tangent to $\\omega$ at $T$.\n\nConsequently the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, as required. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.  ∎"
