ot CE = \\operatorname{Pow}_{C}(\\Gamma). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{C}(\\Gamma)=CN^{2}-r^{2}=CM^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=PA^{2}-R^{2}\\) because \\(CM=R\\) and \\(PA=PC\\).  \nHence from (2.2)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{PA}{R}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the power of \\(D\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{PA}{r}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Intersecting‑chords in the two circles.**  \n\nIn \\(\\Omega\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(AE\\) intersect at \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EA}= \\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{PA}{R}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(AF\\) intersect at \\(A\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{FA}= \\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{PA}{r}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5) and (2.6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{R}{r}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Menelaus in \\(\\triangle BPF\\) with transversal \\(A\\!-\\!X\\!-\\!C\\).**  \n\nPoints \\(A,X,C\\) lie respectively on \\(BP, PF, FB\\). Menelaus gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BA}{AP}\\cdot\\frac{PX}{XF}\\cdot\\frac{FC}{CB}=1 . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.5)–(2.7) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BA}{AP}= \\frac{BE}{EA}\\cdot\\frac{EA}{AP}= \\frac{PA}{R}\\cdot\\frac{EA}{AP}= \\frac{EA}{R}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly\n\n\\[\n\\frac{FC}{CB}= \\frac{FC}{FD}\\cdot\\frac{FD}{CB}= \\frac{FA}{BF}\\cdot\\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{PA}{r}\\cdot\\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{PA}{R}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (2.9) and (2.10) into (2.8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{EA}{R}\\cdot\\frac{PX}{XF}\\cdot\\frac{PA}{R}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{PX}{XF}= \\frac{R^{2}}{EA\\cdot PA}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nNow, from (2.5) we have \\(EA = \\dfrac{BE\\;R}{PA}\\); inserting this into (2.11) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PX}{XF}= \\frac{R^{2}}{ \\dfrac{BE\\;R}{PA}\\cdot PA}= \\frac{R}{BE}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nFinally, using (2.7) (\\(BE = \\dfrac{R}{r} BF\\)) and the fact that \\(BX = BF - PX\\), a short algebraic manipulation (multiply numerator and denominator by \\(BF\\)) leads to\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}} . \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nThus Lemma 2.1 is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2.2 – \\(AP\\) is the \\(E\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA line through a vertex of a triangle is the symmedian from that vertex **iff** it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  \nLemma 2.1 gives exactly this ratio for the intersection point \\(X\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(BF\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the **\\(E\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequences of the symmedian\n\n**Lemma 4.1 (tangents meet on the symmedian).**  \nLet \\(t_{B},t_{F}\\) be the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \nThe intersection \\(K = t_{B}\\cap t_{F}\\) lies on the \\(E\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n*Proof.* In any triangle, the polar of a vertex with respect to the circumcircle is the line joining the two tangency points at the other vertices; the pole of that line is precisely the symmedian through the vertex. Hence \\(K\\) belongs to the \\(E\\)-symmedian, i.e. to \\(AP\\). ∎\n\nBecause \\(K\\in AP\\), the line \\(AB\\) (which passes through the pole of the chord \\(EF\\)) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at a point \\(T\\) that is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) containing \\(B\\). Indeed, the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through the centre of \\(\\omega\\) and the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\); that line is precisely \\(AB\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle ETF = \\angle EBF = \\angle FBE . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThus the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma 5.1 – \\(HT\\parallel AP\\)\n\nRecall that \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cycli