-\n### 4.  Consequences of the symmedian\n\n**Lemma 4.1 (tangents meet on the symmedian).**  \nLet \\(t_{B},t_{F}\\) be the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \nThe intersection \\(K = t_{B}\\cap t_{F}\\) lies on the \\(E\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n*Proof.* In any triangle, the polar of a vertex with respect to the circumcircle is the line joining the two tangency points at the other vertices; the pole of that line is precisely the symmedian through the vertex. Hence \\(K\\) belongs to the \\(E\\)-symmedian, i.e. to \\(AP\\). ∎\n\nBecause \\(K\\in AP\\), the line \\(AB\\) (which passes through the pole of the chord \\(EF\\)) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at a point \\(T\\) that is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) containing \\(B\\). Indeed, the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through the centre of \\(\\omega\\) and the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\); that line is precisely \\(AB\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle ETF = \\angle EBF = \\angle FBE . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThus the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma 5.1 – \\(HT\\parallel AP\\)\n\nRecall that \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(PH\\) with\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPC = \\angle HPD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5.1) and (5.2) we obtain that \\(PH\\) bisects \\(\\angle APH\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle APH . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nNow, because \\(AP\\) is the \\(E\\)-symmedian, the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\) coincides with \\(AP\\). The internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\) is therefore the line \\(AB\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle EAF . \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nUsing the cycl