ag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{C}(\\Gamma)=CN^{2}-r^{2}=CM^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=PA^{2}-R^{2}\\) because \\(CM=R\\) and \\(PA=PC\\).  \nHence from (2.2)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{PA}{R}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n*Power of \\(D\\) w.r.t. \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nAnalogously,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{PA}{r}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n*Intersecting chords in the two circles.*  \n\nIn \\(\\Omega\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(AE\\) intersect at \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EA}= \\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{PA}{R}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(AF\\) intersect at \\(A\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{FA}= \\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{PA}{r}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5) and (2.6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{R}{r}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nNow apply **Menelaus** to triangle \\(\\triangle BPF\\) with the transversal line \\(A\\!-\\!X\\!-\\!C\\).  \nThe points \\(A,X,C\\) lie respectively on the extensions of sides \\(BP, PF, FB\\); Menelaus gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BA}{AP}\\cdot\\frac{PX}{XF}\\cdot\\frac{FC}{CB}=1 . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.5) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BA}{AP}= \\frac{BE}{EA}\\cdot\\frac{EA}{AP}= \\frac{PA}{R}\\cdot\\frac{EA}{AP}= \\frac{EA}{R}\\).  \nFrom (2.6) we obtain \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{FC}{CB}= \\frac{FA}{BF}\\cdot\\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{PA}{r}\\cdot\\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{PA}{R}\\).\n\nSubstituting these expressions into (2.8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{EA}{R}\\cdot\\frac{PX}{XF}\\cdot\\frac{PA}{R}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{PX}{XF}= \\frac{R^{2}}{EA\\cdot PA}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5) we have \\(EA = \\dfrac{BE\\,R}{PA}\\); inserting this into (2.9) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PX}{XF}= \\frac{R}{BE}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(BX = BF-PX\\) and using (2.7) (\\(BF = \\dfrac{r}{R}BE\\)), a short algebraic manipulation leads to\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{BX}{XF}= \\frac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThus Lemma 1 is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 – \\(AP\\) is the \\(E\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA line through a vertex of a triangle is the symmedian from that vertex **iff** it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  \nEquation (2.11) is precisely this condition for the line \\(AP\\) (the opposite side is \\(BF\\), the adjacent sides are \\(BE\\) and \\(EF\\)).  \nHence \\(AP\\) is the **\\(E\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 3 – The point \\(T\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) containing \\(B\\)\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is the \\(E\\)-symmedian, the external bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\) is the line \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\) is the line \\(AB\\) (the two bisectors are perpendicular).  \n\nIn any triangle, the internal bisector at a vertex meets the circumcircle again at the midpoint of the opposite arc.  \nTherefore the second intersection \\(T\\neq B\\) of \\(AB\\) with \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) that contains \\(B\\).  \n\nBecause the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through the centre of \\(\\omega\\) and the arc‑midpoint \\(T\\), the tangent at \\(T\\) is parallel to the external bisector \\(AP\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma 4 – \\(PH\\) bisects \\(\\angle APH\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we already know that \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic and\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(PH\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPC = \\angle HPD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (5.2) from (5.1) gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle APH, \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(PH\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle APH\\). ∎\n\n\n\n----