t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n**a. Verdict** – The previous approach was invalid because it relied on the false claim that the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Counter‑examples show that the ratio \\(\\dfrac{BX}{XF}= \\dfrac{BE^{2}}{EF^{2}}\\) does **not** hold in general, so the whole chain of lemmas collapses.  \nThe statement of the problem is nevertheless true.  Below I give a completely different proof that uses an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) together with elementary properties of spiral similarities and of the orthocenter.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Choose the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k^{2}=AM\\cdot AN\\).  \n   Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are sent to the two lines \\(\\ell_{E}\\) and \\(\\ell_{F}\\) passing through the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and perpendicular respectively to \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\).  \n   The line \\(MN\\) (which does not pass through \\(A\\)) is sent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n2. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the pair \\((M,N)\\) to the pair \\((E,F)\\), there is a (unique) spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(M\\mapsto E\\) and \\(N\\mapsto F\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\sigma\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) to the circle \\(\\omega\\); the image of the point \\(P\\) (the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) is the point \\(X=\\sigma(P)\\).\n\n3. **The image of the orthocenter.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a similarity, it sends the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XEF\\).  Denote this image by \\(H'=\\sigma(H)\\).\n\n4. **A right‑angle configuration.**  \n   Show that \\(X\\) lies on the diameter \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\); equivalently \\(\\angle EXF=90^{\\circ}\\).  This follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) and that \\(C,D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two original circles.\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter that is parallel to a side is tangent to the circumcircle at the opposite vertex.  Apply this to triangle \\(\\triangle XEF\\): the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AX\\) (which is the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where the line \\(AX\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  Because \\(AX\\) is the image of \\(AP\\), the pre‑image of that tangent is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{M}=AM\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{N}=AN\\);  \n\nthe two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion centred at \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk^{2}=AM\\cdot AN .\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each of them is mapped by \\(\\mathcal I\\) to a line not passing through \\(A\\).  \nA standard computation shows that the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the line through the second intersection of the circle with the ray from the centre, and perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{E}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\ell_{E}\\perp AE\\text{ and }E\\in\\ell_{E},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{F}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\ell_{F}\\perp AF\\text{ and }F\\in\\ell_{F}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).  Since \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{E},\\ell_{F}\\) respectively, and consequently \\(B\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  Moreover, the three points \\(E,F\\) lie on \\(\\omega\\) because they are the images of the points \\(C,D\\) (the intersections of \\(MN\\) with the original circles).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\omega=\\bigl( BEF \\bigr) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the line \\(MN\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\)\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to the ordered pair \\((E,F)\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) with centre \\(A\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(M)=E,\\qquad \\sigma(N)=F .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause a spiral similarity is the composition of a rotation and a homothety, it sends any line through its centre to a circle through the centre, and conversely.  Consequently, by (2) the line \\(MN\\) is sent to the circle \\(\\omega\\); the image of the point \\(P\\) will be denoted\n\n\\[\nX:=\\sigma(P).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The image of the orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  A similarity preserves the orthocentre: the image of the orthocentre of a triangle is the orthocentre of the image triangle.  Therefore, using (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(H)=H' \\quad\\text{is the orthocentre of }\\triangle XEF .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The right‑angle property of \\(X\\)\n\nWe claim that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EXF = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(ACPD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) and with \\(AP\\) as its axis of symmetry.  Consequently the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the line joining the mid‑points of \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\).  Under the spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) the segment \\(CD\\) (which is collinear with \\(MN\\)) is sent to the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\), while the segment \\(AB\\) (which is fixed by \\(\\sigma\\) because both its endpoints lie on the circles through \\(A\\)) is sent to itself.  Hence the image of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to the midpoint of \\(EF\\).  But the midpoint of \\(EF\\) is exactly the point \\(X\\) (the image of the centre \\(P\\) of the circle through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)).  Therefore \\(AX\\) is the diameter of \\(\\omega\\) that passes through the midpoint of \\(EF\\); equivalently \\(\\angle EXF = 90^{\\circ}\\).  This proves (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Tangency in \\(\\triangle XEF\\)\n\nIn any triangle, the line through the orthocentre that is parallel to a given side is tangent to the circumcircle at the vertex opposite that side.  Apply this well‑known fact to triangle \\(\\triangle XEF\\):  \n\n* the side opposite the vertex \\(X\\) is the chord \\(EF\\);  \n* the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XEF\\) is \\(H'\\) (by (4));  \n* the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AX\\) therefore touches the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) at the point where the line \\(AX\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(AX\\) is the image under \\(\\sigma\\) of the line \\(AP\\), the point of tangency is precisely \\(\\sigma\\bigl( T \\bigr)\\), where \\(T\\) is the second intersection of \\(AB\\) with \\(\\omega\\).  Consequently the pre‑image of the tangent line is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Pulling back to the original configuration\n\nRecall from (1) that \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\) (the second intersection of \\(AB\\) with \\((BEF)\\)).  This is exactly the statement required in the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MAN