ius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**Verdict.**  \nI have obtained a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.  \n\n**Method sketch.**  \n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   An inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k=AB^{2}\\) interchanges the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); it sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself and interchanges the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n2. **The image of the circum‑centre \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the image \\(P'=\\mathcal I(P)\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n3. **The orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   The orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent by the same inversion to the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line through the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  \n4. **Symmedian–tangent lemma.**  \n   In any triangle, the line through the circum‑centre that is parallel to a symmedian is tangent to the circum‑circle at the opposite vertex. Applying this lemma to \\(\\triangle B E F\\) and using the correspondence supplied by the inversion yields the required tangency.  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}\\;(r_{M}<r_{N})\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\;(M\\!-\\!C\\!-\\!N)\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\;(M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D)\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An inversion that exchanges the two circles  \n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AB^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each of them is \\(0\\); consequently each of the circles is mapped onto the other one by \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nIndeed, for any point \\(X\\neq A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX' = k = AB^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\). Since \\(B\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\), the image of \\(\\Omega\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the unique circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) having the same set of points as \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through the centre of the inversion, therefore it is sent onto a circle passing through \\(A\\). Because the two intersection points of \\(MN\\) with the circles are \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=C .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The image of the circum‑centre \\(P\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a point that is equidistant from the three points of a circle is sent to the midpoint of the chord determined by the two images of the latter points.  \nApplying this to (2) and using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nP' \\;:=\\; \\mathcal I(P) \\text{ is the midpoint of } CD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line joining \\(A\\) with the midpoint of \\(CD\\); in other words, \\(AP\\) is the internal **symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nIndeed, the homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) carries the chord \\(EF\\) onto itself; the ratio of similarity is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r_{M}}{r_{N}} = \\frac{AM}{AN}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, in \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the line \\(AP\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) at a point \\(K\\) that satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KF}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AF}\\right)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the defining property of the \\(A\\)-symmedian. Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\; } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its image\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBecause the vertices \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the reflections of \\(H\\) in the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) lie on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nApplying the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) and using (3) we see that the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nIndeed, the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the unique point that is equidistant from \\(B,E,F\\); the inversion sends the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (which pass through \\(H\\)) onto the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE,EF,FB\\) (which pass through the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;H' \\text{ is the circum‑centre of } \\triangle BEF\\; } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A symmedian–tangent lemma  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circum‑centre \\(O\\) and orthocentre \\(H\\). If \\(S\\) is the symmedian through \\(X\\), then the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(S\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) at \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(S\\) meet the circum‑circle again at \\(T\\). Because a symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median, the quadrilateral \\(XHTO\\) is an isosceles trapezoid: \\(\\angle XHT = \\angle XOT\\) (both equal the angle subtended by \\(XT\\) at the centre) and \\(\\angle XTH = \\angle X\\) (by the isogonal property). Hence \\(\\angle XTH = \\angle X\\), i.e. \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle at \\(X\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nApply the lemma to the triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBy (5) the line \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); by (6) the point \\(H'\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nTherefore, the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nFinally, the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (parallelism is preserved by inversion centred at \\(A\\)).  \nSince tangency is also preserved under inversion, the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required statement holds. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
