e midpoint of the chord determined by the two images of the latter points.  \nApplying this to (2) and using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nP' \\;:=\\; \\mathcal I(P) \\text{ is the midpoint of } CD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line joining \\(A\\) with the midpoint of \\(CD\\); in other words, \\(AP\\) is the internal **symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nIndeed, the homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) carries the chord \\(EF\\) onto itself; the ratio of similarity is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r_{M}}{r_{N}} = \\frac{AM}{AN}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, in \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the line \\(AP\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) at a point \\(K\\) that satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KF}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AF}\\right)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the defining property of the \\(A\\)-symmedian. Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\; } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its image\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBecause the vertices \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the reflections of \\(H\\) in the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) lie on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nApplying the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) and using (3) we see that the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nIndeed, the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the unique point that is equidistant from \\(B,E,F\\); the inversion sends the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (which pass through \\(H\\)) onto the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE,EF,FB\\) (which pass through the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;H' \\text{ is the circum‑centre of } \\triangle BEF\\; } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n------------------------------------------------------