"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proven the key equality  \n\\[\nHE=HF,\n\\]  \ni.e. the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\).  \nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(EF\\), this equality implies that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n  Place the configuration in the Cartesian plane with  \n  \\[\n  A=(0,0),\\qquad P=(p,0),\\qquad E=(e,0),\\qquad F=(f,0),\n  \\]  \n  where the \\(x\\)\u2013axis is the line \\(AP\\).  \n  The centres of the given circles are then  \n  \\[\n  M=\\Bigl(\\frac e2,\\;y_M\\Bigr),\\qquad \n  N=\\Bigl(\\frac f2,\\;y_N\\Bigr),\n  \\]  \n  because each centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AE\\) (resp. \\(AF\\)).  \n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 Determining the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).**  \n  The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have equations  \n  \\[\n  (x-\\tfrac e2)^2+(y-y_M)^2=\\Bigl(\\tfrac e2\\Bigr)^2+y_M^{\\,2},\n  \\qquad\n  (x-\\tfrac f2)^2+(y-y_N)^2=\\Bigl(\\tfrac f2\\Bigr)^2+y_N^{\\,2}.\n  \\]  \n  The line \\(MN\\) has equation  \n  \\[\n  y-y_M=\\frac{2(y_N-y_M)}{\\,f-e\\,}\\Bigl(x-\\frac e2\\Bigr).\n  \\]  \n  Substituting this line into the two circle equations yields the two intersection points\n  \\[\n  C=\\bigl(c,\\,y_C\\bigr),\\qquad D=\\bigl(d,\\,y_D\\bigr),\n  \\]  \n  where \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  (2p-e)c=2y_Cy_M,\\qquad (2p-f)d=2y_Dy_N .\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Using that \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n  Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(p\\); hence\n  \\[\n  c^{2}+y_C^{2}=2pc,\\qquad d^{2}+y_D^{2}=2pd .\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]  \n  Eliminating \\(y_C\\) and \\(y_D\\) from (1) and (2) gives\n  \\[\n  p=\\frac{e+f}{2}.\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n  The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  Solving the two linear equations gives\n  \\[\n  H=\\Bigl(\\frac{e+f}{2},\\,\\frac{y_M+y_N}{2}\\Bigr).\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]  \n  In particular the \\(x\\)\u2013coordinate of \\(H\\) is \\(\\dfrac{e+f}{2}\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f5 \u2013 Equality \\(HE=HF\\).**  \n  Because \\(E=(e,0)\\) and \\(F=(f,0)\\), from (4) we obtain\n  \\[\n  HE^{2}=(\\tfrac{e+f}{2}-e)^{2}+\\bigl(\\tfrac{y_M+y_N}{2}\\bigr)^{2},\n  \\qquad\n  HF^{2}=(\\tfrac{e+f}{2}-f)^{2}+\\bigl(\\tfrac{y_M+y_N}{2}\\bigr)^{2}.\n  \\]  \n  The two expressions are equal, whence\n  \\[\n  HE=HF .\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n* **Step\u202f6 \u2013 Tangency.**  \n  Equality (5) means that \\(H\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the \\(x\\)\u2013axis) is perpendicular to the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) drawn to the point where this line meets the circle.  Hence that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
